Rem ==================================================================
Rem seedkeys.shoddy — reckoner seed: keys.shoddy, key codes named
Rem
Rem THIS SEED EXISTS BECAUSE A NOTE WAS WRONG. halifax-core.shoddy
Rem listed keys.shoddy among the machines a prompt could not reach,
Rem under "raw keystrokes, which InputLine already owns" — the same
Rem live-resource heading as the windows and the sockets. It is not one.
Rem keys.shoddy reads no keyboard and owns no device: ClassifyKey takes
Rem a NUMBER and answers which key that code stands for, and KeyGlyph
Rem and IsKey are questions about that answer. The whole machine is a
Rem lookup table. Whatever produced the code — a scribbler event, a log
Rem file, a person typing 262 at a prompt — is somebody else's business.
Rem
Rem So there was never a rule in the way, only a misfiling, and what a
Rem session gets is the plainly useful half: "what is key code 262?"
Rem
Rem GAMEKEY DOES NOT CROSS THE BRIDGE, and does not need to. Per R4.16
Rem there is no KEY cell, and a Type with eleven cases would be a poor
Rem one — every question a caller asks of a GameKey is answered by a
Rem STRING or a BOOLEAN already. So the three words take the CODE and
Rem answer those directly, and ClassifyKey stays inside.
Rem
Rem KEYNAME ANSWERS THE GLYPH FOR A LETTER OR DIGIT, and the key's name
Rem otherwise. That is one word rather than two because it is one
Rem question — "which key is this?" — and "char" would be an answer
Rem that made the caller ask again.
Rem ==================================================================

Include "keys.shoddy"
Include "cuttle.shoddy"
Include "reckoner.shoddy"
Include "seq.shoddy"

Rem ---- naming a key ------------------------------------------------------

Rem The named keys spell themselves; a letter or digit answers its own
Rem glyph, so 65 is "A" and 262 is "left". Anything the table does not
Rem know is "other", which is a real answer and not a failure: the
Rem ScribblerKeys range is wider than the keys with names.
Def KsName(k As GameKey) As String
    Select Case k
        Case ArrowLeft
            "left"
        Case ArrowRight
            "right"
        Case ArrowUp
            "up"
        Case ArrowDown
            "down"
        Case SpaceBar
            "space"
        Case EnterKey
            "enter"
        Case EscKey
            "esc"
        Case TabKey
            "tab"
        Case BackKey
            "back"
        Case Else
            KsGlyphOr(k)

Def KsGlyphOr(k As GameKey) As String
    If KeyGlyph(k) = "" Then
        "other"
    Else
        KeyGlyph(k)

Rem ---- the words ---------------------------------------------------------

Def KsSeedName(st As RckState) As RckState
    Let s1 = RckReg(st, "KEYNAME", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => KsNameOf(vs)), "( code -- str )", "Which key a physical key code stands for: \"left\", \"right\", \"up\", \"down\", \"space\", \"enter\", \"esc\", \"tab\", \"back\", the glyph itself for a letter or digit, or \"other\".")
    Let s2 = RckReg(s1, "KEYGLYPH", 1, RckPure(Fn(vs) => KsGlyphOf(vs)), "( code -- str )", "The character a letter or digit key stands for, and \"\" for every named key and anything unmapped. KEYNAME is the one that names the rest.")
    RckReg(s2, "KEYIS", 2, RckPure(Fn(vs) => KsIsOf(vs)), "( code s -- flag )", "Whether that code is the letter or digit s. Case does not matter, because a physical letter key always reports uppercase.")

Rem A key code is a whole number and nothing else. RckWholeArg refuses
Rem negatives and fractions already, which is every way of mistyping one
Rem that the table could otherwise answer "other" to and look right.
Def KsNameOf(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckWholeArg("KEYNAME", First(vs))
        Case Ok(code)
            RckVals({ CutStr(KsName(ClassifyKey(code))) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("KEYNAME did not answer")

Def KsGlyphOf(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case RckWholeArg("KEYGLYPH", First(vs))
        Case Ok(code)
            RckVals({ CutStr(KeyGlyph(ClassifyKey(code))) })
        Case Err(w, at)
            RckWorkBad(w)
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("KEYGLYPH did not answer")

Def KsIsOf(vs As List Of Cell) As RckWork
    Select Case Pair(RckWholeArg("KEYIS", Nth(vs, 1)), RckTextArg("KEYIS", Nth(vs, 2)))
        Case Pair(Ok(code), Ok(s))
            RckVals({ CutBool(IsKey(ClassifyKey(code), s)) })
        Case Else
            RckWorkBad("KEYIS needs a key code and a STRING")

Rem ---- the seed ----------------------------------------------------------

Def RckSeedKeys(st As RckState) As RckState
    KsSeedName(RckSeeding(st, "seed-keys"))
